The idea is that you subscribe in your [`Application`](https://docs.rs/iced/0.1.1/iced/trait.Application.html#method.subscription) to subscriptions you want to listen to which then kick of messages you can deal with in your [`update`](https://docs.rs/iced/0.1.1/iced/trait.Application.html#tymethod.update) function. The [`download_progress`](https://github.com/hecrj/iced/tree/master/examples/download_progress) example subscribes to a stream of download events like this:
```rust
fn subscription(&self) -> Subscription<Message> {
  match self {
    Example::Downloading { .. } => {
      download::file("https://speed.hetzner.de/100MB.bin")
               .map(Message::DownloadProgressed)
      }
      _ => Subscription::none(),
  }
}
```
The magic happens in [`download.rs`](https://github.com/hecrj/iced/blob/master/examples/download_progress/src/download.rs) where we create a [`Recipe`](https://docs.rs/iced_native/0.2.2/iced_native/subscription/trait.Recipe.html) which *creates* the stream.

Because you already have Stream just wrapping it into a Recipe and mapping it to an Message should be all you need. Much fun!

