Issue title: Allow multiple read/write calls per transaction via SPI/I2C

[TARGET]
Ooo! I like the idea of something that actually works, and I'm really comfortable with the look of option number 2 it as it maps to other builder interfaces I've used.

I'll try to counter argue how we might benefit more from having an abstraction that owns the bus address / select lines. I've been mulling it over, and we don't need to break existing drivers and the porting effort would (hopefully) be minimal and beneficial.

In the end, this is a good thought exercise and as long as the community benefits it's all fun for me! I don't mind throwing my work out if it's not the right path.

Let's say we have a few abstractions:

```
I2CProxy(u16)
SPIProxy(OutputPin)
OneWireProxy(u64)

LegacySPIProxy(OutputPin)
LegacyI2CProxy(u16)
```

We should be able to have the `Legacy` flavors provide the familiar interface but no-op the addresses that are sent by the `address` parameter of `read()` and `write()`. This would allow backward compatibility with a new system while others opt-in to the new interface. The back end of this new system would ideally start by consuming the existing APIs provided by the device crates, but overtime implementations that work closer to the metal would surface. For i2c, it also allows for devices with multiple addresses to be selected by the consumer.

In the end, i2c, SPI, serial and 1-wire are all transferring either by full or half duplex. I like the idea of having a consistent experience across bus types even if access patterns under the hood are different.

I'm not aware of any devices that require two distinct addresses / busses. If anyone has one, that'll be a great curve ball to think through.

```
//! Device / Driver consumer examples for the legacy case

// SPI Example
let bus_master = BusManager::new_spi(spi);
let select_expander = some_device::gimme_pin();
let bus_node = bus_master::legacy_spi(select_expander);
let stretch_armstrong = Mcp3008::new(bus_node, BusManager::dummy_pin());

// I2C Example
let bus_master = BusManager::new_i2c(i2c);
let bus_node = bus_master::legacy_i2c(Mag3110::ADDRESS);
let magna_doodle = Mag3110::new(bus_node);

// Multiple Light Sensors
let bus_master = BusManager::new_i2c(i2c);
let base_address = Tsl2561::BASE_ADDRESS;
let light_bright1 = Tsl2561::new(bus_master::legacy_i2c(base_address), 0x00);
let light_bright2 = Tsl2561::new(bus_master::legacy_i2c(base_address+0x10), 0x00);
let light_bright3 = Tsl2561::new(bus_master::legacy_i2c(base_address+0x20), 0x00);
```

